/** siman 467 batch A — chunk 8: machatzit-hashekel (57), peri-megadim (41) */
export const CHUNK8 = {
  "machatzit-hashekel:1:_":
    "In the Tur he wrote 26 and some say perhaps he will grind them, etc., for one does not buy flour from the market, etc., and the Ran who wrote that even if the splitting is recognizable it is forbidden to sell them to a gentile lest he sell them to an Israelite and the Israelite not intend to examine them. Or lest the gentile grind them and return and sell them to an Israelite — this is because the Ran wrote before this according to the Rif regarding guarding from harvest time in the passage \"and a person needs to guard, etc.\" in the name of a Gaon that one does not buy flour from the market except in pressing circumstances. And this is only for matzah of the commandment; but other bread that a person eats on Pesach, etc. — it implies that it is permitted to buy flour from the market for other matzot. But the Tur — it is possible he holds that even for other matzot one does not buy from the market, for now the practice of gentiles is to moisten the wheat before grinding; and with this what Kaf HaChayyim questioned is not difficult at all, that the Israelite will take the flour and leave it in his house and make a prohibition in that he leaves chametz in his house, and even if it was nullified nevertheless there is a d'rabbanan prohibition — no difficulty here, for it is forbidden to make matzot from them; and it is a distant concern that an Israelite will buy flour about which there is concern for leavening, for from this reason he is not permitted to make from them since their practice is to moisten them; and from the Torah he will buy this flour and leave it without need; and it is possible that according to what is forbidden to make matzot from them for all the days of Pesach, since their practice is to moisten, it is possible that also to leave it in his house is forbidden; in any case it is a distant concern, end quote.",

  "machatzit-hashekel:2:א":
    "(seif katan 2) For if so, on the eve of Pesach at the fifth hour it is forbidden, for if he sells it then to an Israelite he will not finish it before Pesach:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ב":
    "And they challenged Rava from the mishnah: a garment in which kilayim was lost — behold it may not be sold to a gentile lest he sell it to an Israelite; since the kilayim are not recognizable — the same applies here to these grains:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ג":
    "For one does not buy bread, etc., and for us see below in seif katan 7:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ד":
    "In the incident in Pesachim page 40b regarding a ship of wheat that sank in the river — at first Rava also thought to sell them to a gentile, and Rava challenged him from this baraita of kilayim and retracted and permitted selling little by little as written in Shulchan Aruch; if so he already knew from the baraita of kilayim — how did he err a second time in the incident of stam yayin:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ה":
    "If so, why did he trouble them, etc. Granted, if both incidents did not come before Rava at one time — there is no difficulty why he did not permit in this incident of stam yayin to sell them little by little, for it is possible that at that time this permission did not occur to him and did not enter his mind; for such is the way of people that many times they understand things that previously did not enter their mind. But according to what Tosafos said that at one time he ruled on both incidents — since he ruled in this incident of chametz to sell them little by little, why did he trouble them in this incident of stam yayin and not rule for them also to sell little by little:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ו":
    "And it implies, etc., even one egg — for such is the language of the Gemara and the codifiers: one does not sell an egg of nevelah or tereifah to a gentile — they used the singular egg and not eggs plural. It implies there is no remedy to sell them little by little; and that which they did not give a remedy to grind them or bake them — it is possible that in their place the practice was to eat gentile bread; and then there is no remedy except to sell to a gentile a slice of bread as the Rama wrote at the end of siman 134 in Yoreh De'ah; and it is possible the wheat was abundant and it was not possible to sell so many slices, for buyers for slices of bread are not easily found; and to leave them a long time is forbidden because of spoilage:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ז":
    "And that which the Rama wrote, etc., in stam yayin that became mixed in water — for stam their wine is nullified in six parts water and then it is permitted even for drinking; and if it became mixed in water and there is not enough to nullify the wine — one sells them to a gentile except for the value of the stam yayin in it; and the Rama concluded: however one may not sell much together, etc. — it implies that little he does not have; if so it is proven that even in other prohibitions little by little is permitted:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ח":
    "They will taste the taste of wine. And even so, if an Israelite tastes them he will make a prohibition for the Israelite. This is not so, for one cannot say the gentile will sell it to an Israelite — the gentile knows if an Israelite tastes them he will perceive the taste of wine and the water will return to him, for he will claim it is a mistaken purchase. And even if you say nevertheless we are concerned — nevertheless this taste is forbidden only d'rabbanan, so there is no reason to be concerned for such a distant concern in a d'rabbanan prohibition:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ט":
    "Rather his intent, etc., is that there are two remedies in the matter: one, the remedy of R' Shimon ben Gamliel mentioned in tractate Avodah Zarah page 74 — that one sells all of it to a gentile except for the value of the stam yayin in it. And the second, the remedy of R' Eliezer explained on page 49b — that one brings the benefit to the Salt Sea; see there in Yoreh De'ah siman 134 in the Tur and Beit Yosef and the distinction between these two remedies. For if one sells them except for the value of the stam yayin in it, one must sell everything at once; but through the remedy of bringing benefit to the Salt Sea one may sell them little by little; and for this R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim concluded: however one may not sell together; and that which the Rama concluded there lest he return and sell it to an Israelite is not from the words of R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim but the Rama added this from his own understanding according to the Rama's understanding. But Magen Avraham holds that what R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim wrote \"however one may not sell together\" means it is not permitted except through throwing benefit into the Salt Sea; and through that one is permitted to sell little by little. But the remedy to sell specifically all together — namely to sell it except for the value of the stam yayin in it — we do not hold this leniency (and according to the language of R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim that Rabbeinu Yosef brought there siman 134 it cannot be understood thus, and the text of R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim is not clear). But also his words require study, for Tosafos at the beginning of chapter 3 of Avodah Zarah, etc., means that R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim wrote thus in the name of Tosafos; and Tosafos' intent is not as R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim understood, for in Avodah Zarah page 49b: this man in whom a barrel of stam yayin became mixed in his donkey-loads came before R' Chiyya; he said to him: take four zuz and throw them in the river and I will permit you. And Tosafos wrote — and this is its language: take, etc., and sell them only by itself — he told him thus as I explained, end quote; and R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim understood that Tosafos' intent is specifically to sell them only by itself — namely through the remedy that one throws the value of the stam yayin — namely four zuz — and it is permitted for him; but he was not permitted to sell them at once through the remedy of except for the value of the stam yayin in it; and on this Magen Avraham wrote: on the contrary, Tosafos' intent is the reverse — for certainly if he wishes to sell them at once except for the stam yayin it is permitted; rather R' Chiyya came to be lenient for him and said throw four zuz in the river and through that he is permitted to sell little by little only by itself. But in the matter, etc., means according to what Magen Avraham explained the words of R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim — if so there is no proof from the words of R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim to permit in other prohibitions to sell little by little lest he sell it to an Israelite; rather stam yayin is different, for an Israelite will not buy wine from a gentile except in pressing circumstances — unlike Shach in Yoreh De'ah siman 84 seif katan 17 who permitted also in other prohibitions to sell to a gentile little by little; see Peri Chadash. And see Yoreh De'ah siman 112 seif 1 who wrote: bread of an Israelite that a gentile baked without Israelite assistance is forbidden, and even to sell it to a gentile is forbidden in a place where they are accustomed to eat gentile bread lest he return and sell it to an Israelite — unless he cuts it into slices, for slices of bread are forbidden to buy from a gentile. It implies that even little by little it is forbidden to sell to a gentile in other prohibitions — since they needed to give the remedy to cut the bread into slices and did not write to sell little by little:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:י":
    "For it would be safek sefeika — meaning since he holds that there should be safek sefeika, it is like nullifying a prohibition l'chatchila:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:כ":
    "And it requires study, for here it deals with much chametz — for little by little is truly permitted; and therefore what he questions — behold it is safek sefeika — is to permit selling much together. And if so he used the safek that perhaps an Israelite will eat it before Pesach since it is much and will not finish before Pesach; for if it is possible it will finish before Pesach it is called little by little — it appears at first glance that he holds like the explanation of Rambam that if he holds like what Magen Avraham wrote that little by little is permitted — that even if he sells it to an Israelite the Israelite will finish it before Pesach — if so even little by little is not permitted except from the aspect of safek sefeika that perhaps he will not sell it to an Israelite, and even if he sells it perhaps the Israelite will finish it before Pesach; and he holds one may not make safek sefeika in one's hands. Or he holds like the explanation of Rambam that little by little is permitted, for we say he bought it for his own need and will not sell it at all; and according to this, for Magen Avraham who holds little by little is permitted because of safek sefeika — it must be said it is permitted to make safek sefeika in one's hands and he was not concerned for the questions of R' Yom Tov Lipmann that even much would be permitted lest the gentile sell to many Israelites little by little, for it is possible this is not a common safek — that presumably as he bought much together so he will sell it. And see Yoreh De'ah safek sefeika 57 there Shach in seif katan 91 on what the Rama wrote there: safek tereifah — there are those who permit it even though we hold to forbid; nevertheless it is permitted to sell it to a gentile, etc., since there are those who permit; and Shach wrote one may not be lenient in this except in pressing circumstances, for otherwise there are sides to permit; it implies but in another prohibition, even if there is a dispute in the matter, they do not permit selling it to a gentile; and the reason: even though there is one safek that perhaps the main law is like those who permit, and if you say like those who are stringent — perhaps he will not sell it to an Israelite — nevertheless it is safek sefeika made in one's hands and forbidden, for we do not say because it is safek sefeika of a dispute it is better than other safek sefeika; on the contrary, a dispute-safek is preferable to other safek sefeika; rather Shach's intent is because it is safek sefeika made in one's hands, as Rabbeinu Menachem proved in his questions on the novelties seif 2; and he wondered at what Shach wrote in siman 66 seif katan 10 that from his words it implies the reverse — see there; and see Peri Chadash in his questions general 18:",

  "machatzit-hashekel:3:_":
    "(seif katan 3) For since, etc., specifically regarding, etc., at end of siman 9 and in Magen Avraham there:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:א":
    "(seif katan 4) Lest, etc., even if one made it on Pesach it is permitted to sell it to a gentile, etc., even though it is forbidden to sell to a gentile lest he sell it to an Israelite — and it is that it has not yet leavened; nevertheless since it will leaven we are concerned lest he sell it to an Israelite. One might say from this Magen Avraham did not speak here but comes to inform us of permission for benefit, because before the measure of a mil it has not yet leavened and benefit is permitted; and regarding concern lest he sell it to an Israelite — from this he did not speak here and relied on what he wrote before that according to Rambam it suffices if he sells it to a gentile little by little. And according to Magen Avraham who requires that even if you say he will sell it to an Israelite the Israelite will finish it before Pesach — if so here where he sells it on Pesach he must sell it in a manner where there is no concern he will sell to an Israelite, such as that he grind it immediately before the selling Israelite:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ב":
    "But one does not buy flour from the market. Or he bake immediately — and certainly an Israelite will not buy leavened bread; and in any case, and according to the Tur one might say specifically above where it already leavened and the chametz is not recognizable — therefore they are concerned lest he sell it to an Israelite. But here where it has not yet leavened — if so it is safek sefeika: one, perhaps when it leavens it will be recognizable; and further, perhaps he will not sell it at all to an Israelite; and also see Magen Avraham in seif katan 12:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ג":
    "The incident, etc., a gentile is not believed even when speaking offhandedly:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ד":
    "After he sold them — and all the time they are in the gentile's possession the gentile is believed; but in what he says that he washed them to enhance his purchase — Shach brought there the dispute of the codifiers whether he is believed. But Kaf HaChayyim wrote here that it requires study whether what he says that he already washed them — one might say to enhance his purchase; on the contrary one might say in this his purchase is diminished rather than enhanced:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ה":
    "For after washing they are more beautiful than before washing; if so, if he had said he had not yet washed them — nevertheless they are beautiful on their own account — thus he would have praised his purchase:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ו":
    "As the Bach, etc., it is forbidden to leave them even if there are sixty, for chametz on Pesach in any amount; and even if he transgressed and left them they are forbidden because he transgressed Beit Yosef — meaning the prohibition in any amount is only d'rabbanan, namely for eating; but regarding that he transgressed Beit Yosef — for the chametz is in his possession — such is in the Bach; but we do not hold thus, as explained above siman 447; and so wrote Magen Avraham in this seif katan; and Magen Avraham did not bring all his words but only to learn from his words one law. And it is what he wrote in this seif katan: and one may learn that if, etc., as written in Yoreh De'ah siman 110: things that are not nullified because of their importance — nevertheless if one was lost or one was eaten of them, the remainder are permitted two by two, for there are two sides to permit: one, perhaps that one which was lost was the prohibition and there is no prohibition at all; and if you say the prohibition remained here — nevertheless since they eat two by two, in every eating there is one of them certainly permitted; and as one of them that was permitted, so too the second:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ז":
    "And in my humble opinion safek sefeika 447 in the name of Rashba that nevertheless it is like nullifying a prohibition l'chatchila, and Magen Avraham agreed there that even if he does not forbid more than the amount of removal — therefore it is properly two by two, for in every matzah there is more of the permitted than the prohibition, even though the baking of the permitted with the prohibition is in the same matzah; nevertheless he holds even if he does not do more than the amount of removal — nevertheless the permitted remains more than the prohibition and it is two by two. Unlike for us who hold that in its place it forbids everything; if so you are concerned the prohibition was not lost and the prohibition is in that matzah — if so there is no two by two, for the small amount of prohibition forbade the entire matzah. And according to what is written siman 447 seif 4 seif katan 11 and so safek sefeika 447: since it was already nullified in the majority and permitted to eat, so too before Pesach one may add to it up to sixty and it is not called nullifying a prohibition; and even though on Pesach it returns and revives — therefore he wrote grind them, and flour in flour is wet in wet; and we hold in siman 447 seif 4 that wet in wet does not return and revive; and grinding too is not nullifying a prohibition, for his intent is not to nullify but only to grind for the need of baking, as written above safek sefeika 447; and see there seif 453 and so in Yoreh De'ah siman 109 that flour in flour is called wet in wet:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ח":
    "And one may learn, etc., dry in dry, etc., and so each stands alone and was not baked together:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ט":
    "For the third mixture — and see Shach seif katan 56:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:י":
    "But in wet it does not apply, etc., for once wet is blended — if so everything is forbidden and there is no permitted at all. And so three mixtures do not apply, for even in the third mixture there is certainly prohibition, since it is blended; and the taste of the permitted in the third mixture is because of safek sefeika: one, perhaps nothing of the prohibition fell into that mixture at all; and further, even if it fell perhaps what he eats is the permitted. But in wet that is blended — certainly some of the prohibition fell there and certainly he eats the prohibition:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:5:א":
    "(seif katan 5) We are not concerned that they go, etc. Know that what Rabbeinu Yosef wrote in seif 5: grain, etc., and one need not suckle, etc., and they receive leavening — these are the words of Rashba on what is written in Pesachim page 33a that if he separated terumah of chametz it is not sanctified, as written: you shall give to him and not to his fire. That is specifically when he had no opportunity, such as if it leavened while attached; and Rashba questioned: if so we would not eat matzah, for certainly rain fell on the grains while attached. Therefore he wrote specifically when they dried and one need not return to the ground — then they receive leavening. And in responsum of Me'il Tzedakah siman 69 he challenged Rashba with proofs and wrote: behold Abbaye said page 46a: this colander in which they roast (Rashi: as they dry nuts in an oven) upside down (meaning the mouth of the pot downward) is permitted, for the water coming out of the nuts does not leaven, for because of lifting them they do not leaven; but right-side up, where the mouth of the pot is upward, is forbidden, for water remains inside the pot and leavens. And Rava said even right-side up is permitted, for fruit juice does not leaven — meaning we hold like Rava; nevertheless it is possible Rava also agrees with Abbaye that because of lifting them they do not leaven; rather he added to permit even right-side up because fruit juice does not leaven. And if so one might say that even while the grains still need the ground they nevertheless receive leavening; only they stated generally that because of lifting them they do not leaven. Unless there was pouring rain until the crop bent and lay on the face of the ground — then there is no because of lifting them. Or we saw that it sprouted or split while attached — such is what the Gemara deals with when it said it leavened while attached, such as when we saw it sprouted while attached; but from the ordinary case, even if suckling from the ground is not needed, we are not concerned lest it leavened even if rain fell, for we say because of lifting them it does not leaven. And he wrote one may rely on this ruling, all the more so when it is a time of pressing need — see there where he expanded:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:5:ב":
    "And therefore wrote specifically when they dried and one need not return to the ground — then they receive leavening, as in Me'il Tzedakah responsum siman 69 challenging Rashba:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:א":
    "(seif katan 7) Only, etc., specifically from that which is in barrels; and he wrote in Sefer Ateret Zekenim and in Kaf HaChayyim: specifically when it is in a barrel with pitch — it is nullified in sixty at the time of cooking — meaning even though the honey was not sixty, nevertheless at the time of cooking they add water and there are certainly sixty; therefore they do not permit the honey in its substance even if it is in barrels; and so to make the drink if the honey was not in a barrel — namely that which they bring in small vessels — also forbidden, for even to make a drink they add water — nevertheless there is no sixty, for in those small vessels they increase flour there:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ב":
    "And to eat the honey in its substance in every manner is forbidden — meaning even if the gentile drew it from the hive before us and we knew he did not mix flour in it — it would be permitted by law; nevertheless it is forbidden because it comes to be confused with other honey, as in the Bach:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ג":
    "And it is forbidden to make the drink on Pesach. For on Pesach it is not nullified in sixty, for it is in any amount; and specifically if it was cooked before Pesach and nullified — it does not return and revive on Pesach, for wet does not return and revive — see Taz:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ד":
    "And the Levush, etc., but honey in its substance, etc., forbidden to eat since they did not add water — there is no sixty. And it appears to me one who is lenient has not lost, for according to the majority they mix only a little; and also the honey alone has sixty:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ה":
    "However honey, etc., also this is from the language of the Levush:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ו":
    "He inferred, etc., because that is forbidden, etc., and it is fitting to be stringent, as the Levush wrote above from this; and it appears to me one who is lenient has not lost and one who is stringent — may he be blessed; and it requires study:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ז":
    "Rather that which they bring in chararim, etc., and for the Bach this too is forbidden because it comes to be confused as stated; and the Levush was not concerned for this — see Magen Avraham seif katan 10:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ח":
    "And there are those who practice, etc., and according to the Bach it is forbidden because it comes to be confused; and everything follows the custom. And in any case later authorities wrote that not all honey in all lands is the same — see Kaf HaChayyim and Ateret Zekenim:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:8:א":
    "(seif katan 8) And grapes, etc., they open the unripe cluster that refers to the exodus from Egypt; and they also say Shir HaShirim on Pesach:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:8:ב":
    "Here is a great difficulty: kalish, etc., and even though the verse, etc., they were not stringent from uncertainty — meaning even though above siman 447 seif 4 there regarding bread into which wine fell we are concerned lest crumbs remained, etc., and the same law here — this case is different, for there this safek of crumbs is close to certainty, unlike here where it is a full safek; and in a rabbinic prohibition in any amount they were lenient in safek. And as written in seif 9 in Magen Avraham seif katan 12; and what people practice, etc., and even apples cut with a knife — there is concern they were cut with a chametz knife; and it is explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 96 that apples are sharp things; nevertheless the water is permitted as Magen Avraham wrote above siman 447 seif 8 regarding olive water — see there:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:9:_":
    "(seif katan 9) To leave it, etc., and this is the language of Maharil: Maharan permitted, etc., tsuker that he brought with him from Candia, etc., and this is the intent of the Rama; and tsuker kandil that has no concern for leavening — not our tsuker kandil; and so wrote the Taz — see there:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:10:_":
    "(seif katan 10) Saffron, etc., and so in honey from a hive — meaning the Levush permitted above seif katan 7; and the Bach above was concerned for substitution. But the Bach above was concerned for substitution in honey too even though in other fruits he was not concerned for substitution:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:11:א":
    "(seif katan 11) They are not, etc., for it is fitting — the Rosh, etc., and so ruled the Mechaber: gentile honey one does not retain, etc., and the Rosh wrote the reason that it is not common that flour becomes mixed in it and also it was nullified before Pesach, end of the Rosh's words:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:11:ב":
    "And as written in siman 447, etc., also this is from the words of Darkei Moshe; and so in Shulchan Aruch above siman 447 seif 5: however if something of these things became mixed, etc., and see there in Magen Avraham:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:11:ג":
    "Siman 447 regarding dry meat seif katan 16 in the name of the Bach. And in Yoreh De'ah siman 69 seif 9 in the gloss: if meat was salted and not rinsed in the final rinsing and dried — even if placed in a kli rishon boiling it is permitted, since it dried there are certainly sixty against the blood:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:12:א":
    "(seif katan 12) They did not split open, etc., one authority says it is permitted — meaning Shmuel holds splitting open is specifically required; but Mar Ukva holds even if they did not split open, if when placed on the opening of the roaster they would split open — meaning they are close to splitting — they are forbidden; and since the halachah was not clarified like whom, all that did not split open literally is a safek. It implies if mixed in less than sixty it is forbidden, for then it is a Torah safek — for we hold the halachah is like all who say d'oraisa. See Yoreh De'ah siman 98 regarding a mixture that was poured out and we know the majority is permitted but we are uncertain if there were sixty — then if it is species in its species, which from the Torah is nullified in the majority, there were sixty d'rabbanan to be lenient; and if it is species in its non-species, which from the Torah requires sixty, it is a Torah safek and therefore we follow the safek to stringency and it is forbidden — the same here. For in seif 2 he ruled even if they did not split open it is forbidden, since it rests at ease; and here too the cooked dish rests at ease — why did he be lenient in the cooked dish:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:12:ב":
    "And it is difficult: since, etc., meaning still what he questioned is not resolved; for behold the Taz in Yoreh De'ah and so Magen Avraham above siman 465:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:א":
    "(seif katan 13) They forbid everything, etc., and the same applies to other cooked dishes, etc., even though the first cooked dish was forbidden only through any amount — nevertheless it forbids the other cooked dishes through any amount:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ב":
    "And in the Taz in Yoreh De'ah, etc., one cannot understand his words through purging — I must present his words briefly: in Chullin page 100 Rav taught: once taste was given in a piece, the piece itself becomes nevelah and forbids all the pieces because they are its species — Rav holds species in its species is not nullified, meaning such as when a pot was full of gravy and within the gravy were several pieces of permitted meat, and above the gravy was placed one permitted piece and on that piece a piece of nevelah; and one cannot combine the pieces within the gravy neither for prohibition nor for permission, since that piece on which the nevelah rests is above the gravy. Therefore if the nevelah piece gave taste in that permitted piece it becomes nevelah; and if it stirred again the whole pot until that upper piece too entered the gravy, it forbids all the pieces even though there are sixty against it because they are its species and for R' Yehuda species in its species is not nullified from the Torah. And the Gemara challenges: what is different — taste was given, etc., even if it did not give taste as well, since it stirred the nevelah piece itself forbids the other pieces; and Abbaye answers: when he preceded and removed the prohibition — meaning the nevelah piece before stirring the pot. And Tosafos challenged: still the difficulty remains — why taste was given in the piece; behold the nevelah piece with that upper piece is species in its species and should be forbidden in any amount and forbid the whole pot when stirred — because of any amount. And Rabbenu Tam answered: namely that we say also in other prohibitions a piece that becomes nevelah through any amount — nevertheless since that piece was forbidden only through any amount it does not forbid the other pieces in the pot through any amount, and it itself does not become nevelah; and so the Tur cited in Yoreh De'ah; and from this the Taz derived regarding chametz on Pesach in a piece forbidden through any amount: if that piece fell again elsewhere it does not forbid in any amount — a fortiori, for according to R' Yehuda species in its species from the Torah is not nullified — nevertheless in such a case it does not forbid, all the more so chametz on Pesach where what forbids in any amount is d'rabbanan — all the more so in such a case it should not forbid. And they loaded the Tur's language there and the Taz inferred from his wording: it does not forbid through purging — meaning as the Taz concluded there: specifically if he recognizes that piece and throws it outside — then it does not forbid except through purging — therefore it is permitted as stated. But if he does not recognize the piece, in any case he must nullify the piece itself, for it was forbidden; and also regarding wet in wet — see there:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ג":
    "And so wrote, etc., and even though the piece does not become nevelah — nevertheless the prohibition forbids in any amount — meaning the any amount of absorbed prohibition forbids. And this is the reasoning of Derishah that the Taz brought there; except the Taz rejected his words: if so the Tosafos' difficulties return to their place — why taste was given in the piece; behold the piece is not nevelah through any amount — nevertheless that absorbed any amount should forbid the whole pot when stirred because they are its species; and one might answer in Sefer Nekudot: according to R' Yehuda, since specifically species in its species is not nullified — if so specifically when the prohibition is in its substance then it is called species in its species and forbids in any amount; but when that any amount is absorbed in the piece and another force is mixed in that any amount, it is not species in its species with the pieces in the pot — therefore the any amount does not forbid the pot when stirred. Unlike for us in chametz, where even if not its species, that absorbed any amount forbids — see there:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ד":
    "And there is a practical difference, etc., meaning for us what difference does it make whether the piece became nevelah through any amount and the piece forbids the other pieces, or the any amount within it forbids — certainly for R' Yehuda there is a practical difference as stated; but for us too who do not hold like R' Yehuda there is a practical difference:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ה":
    "A thing that imparts taste, etc., meaning if at first it fell into a thing that imparts taste where sixty does not help and it was forbidden, and afterward that piece fell elsewhere where absorption does not impart taste there — if the piece had become nevelah it would require sixty against the whole piece; but since through any amount it did not become nevelah, it does not need sixty except against the any amount:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ו":
    "Chametz within its time, etc., Magen Avraham explained below his intent in this. If it became mixed in its species and not its species — we say remove, etc. — if so the intent is not as the simple meaning that it fell immediately into its species and not its species; for even though for R' Yehuda species in its species is not nullified, nevertheless we say seif katan: its species is as if it is not its species and what is not its species is greater and nullifies it, as we say in Chullin page 106 that this law has no application here for practical differences. Rather it must be that it fell first into its species alone and forbade it in any amount, and afterward that piece forbidden in any amount fell into not its species — for if the piece had become nevelah it would require sixty against the whole piece, and now that through any amount it did not become nevelah it does not need sixty except against the any amount. And what he concluded remove its species as if it is not — the Gemara used concise language; its intent is that piece forbidden in any amount because it was its species — we say remove, for it does not forbid through purging since it did not become nevelah; therefore he did not conclude in the Gemara's language and what is not its species is greater and nullifies it; but still the difficulty remains why it fell afterward into not its species — even if it fell again into its species, since he certainly holds as stated in Sefer Nekudot that since another force is mixed in it, for R' Yehuda species in its species from the Torah is not nullified by Torah decree — in such a case it is called not its species; and it is a great stretch to say his intent in what he wrote is truly thus — that even its species is called not its species. Rather without that it is strained in my eyes that the practical difference should be as the simple meaning according to R' Yehuda who holds species in its species is not nullified — for we do not hold like him; therefore in my humble opinion it deals with davar sheyeish lo matirin, for we hold species in its species is not nullified and in not its species it is nullified; and accordingly one understands why the Rama follows his view that he wrote in Turei Zahav: the reason they distinguish for davar sheyeish lo matirin between its species and not its species — Shach brought in siman 102 seif katan 4, and this is its language: the importance is not from the prohibition itself but because afterward he will have permission, etc., and whatever became mixed not in its species the permission is not called by the name of the prohibited thing but by the name of what it was mixed into — and it is as if it has no permitters, end quote. And if so in davar sheyeish lo matirin even not its species was not fit to be nullified except because the prohibition is called by the name of the permitted; if so in such a case even the any amount of absorption with another force mixed in is called not its species for R' Yehuda that species in its species from the Torah is not nullified by Torah decree, as stated in Sefer Nekudot; but for davar sheyeish lo matirin, since in any case it is called by the name of the prohibition even if not its species it is not nullified — therefore the Rama needed to say it became mixed afterward in not its species literally; and this is a straight reasoning in my humble opinion. But Sefer Nekudot — it must be according to his own view, for the Bach himself, etc., meaning the Bach too wrote this practical difference that the Rama wrote in glosses Orach Chayyim: chametz within its time, etc.:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ז":
    "In siman 447 — see there in Magen Avraham seif katan 42 who brought it. Permitted; and he wrote further there in Sefer Nekudot as stated that it is not davar sheyeish lo matirin because it returns to its prohibition next year, as written in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah end of siman 102:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ח":
    "He did not look in the Mordechai, etc., they already challenged this above siman 447 seif katan 45 — see there:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ט":
    "And even a piece forbidden because of any amount, etc., all of it is forbidden — meaning not as Sefer Nekudot holds that if it became mixed again in not its species it is nullified, for according to him the reason is davar sheyeish lo matirin that is nullified in not its species; rather even if mixed in its species it is nullified, for according to Sefer Nekudot that even for davar sheyeish lo matirin nevertheless that any amount with another force mixed in is called not its species as stated — if so it is nullified; but according to Magen Avraham that even in not its species nevertheless dry is not nullified — if so even if mixed in not its species literally it is not nullified, for the absorbed any amount is forbidden:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:14:_":
    "(seif katan 14) Permitted, etc., and also the Gemara, etc., because it is d'rabbanan — meaning the last Yom Tov is d'rabbanan, not because there are sixty — Yom Tov against chol:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:15:א":
    "(seif katan 15) All the dough, etc., meaning the Rashba came to resolve: since he does not press more than the amount of removal — if so why do some say all the dough or the matzah — behold it is explained above siman 447: its mixture is like other mixtures, etc., but if in other mixtures one did not need sixty but peeling or removal — so too in chametz; and since here in cold he does not press more than certainly the amount of removal — why did they forbid all according to one authority — on this he brought the Rashba's words and said: but here we find, etc., meaning now it fell and was not at the time of stirring within the dough — for it is explained seif 12 seif katan 18 that from time to time we do not say here it was found, etc.; but in a place of gravy, etc., and the water even though cold he holds the law of gravy applies to them — the Rashba holds that on Pesach even cold forbids, as Magen Avraham wrote in this seif katan:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:15:ב":
    "Nevertheless a small matter, etc., meaning if so one should not forbid because of the last taste — the bread itself leavened the matzah; nevertheless one should forbid because of the first tastes that he wrote:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:16:א":
    "(seif katan 16) Here, etc., now it fell, etc., meaning since it is from place to place we say: once found in matzah we say here it was found in matzah and was never in the dough at all:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:16:ב":
    "If so it appears it was in water, etc., meaning presumably it was in the water with which the dough was kneaded; and the Bach wrote: perhaps now it fell, etc., meaning even if the reasoning of here it was found, etc., was not accepted and one says because of this it certainly fell now — nevertheless one does not emerge from doubt to certainty; and with another safek added it becomes safek sefeika; and there is a practical difference between two reasons whether we permit through safek sefeika or through here it was found, etc. — Magen Avraham explained in this seif katan; but without softening everything is permitted — meaning even in hot, as Magen Avraham wrote seif katan 25 that we hold like Mar Ukva that even if they did not split open but are fit to split open they are forbidden; and since we are not expert whether they are fit to split open — but without softening it is certain they are not fit to split open — therefore it is permitted even for us:",
  "machatzit-hashekel:16:ג":
    "And it implies, etc., and he brought it to another house, etc., for it is from place to place; but if a grain was found in a barrel then it is from time to time. And according to the Bach's view, etc., and this is the practical difference between the Rama's reason and the Bach's reason that it is lack of knowledge — it should be that the safek the Bach wrote that perhaps it did not leaven is lack of knowledge, for one who understands and recognizes knows whether it leavened or not; nevertheless even if it certainly leavened behold there is a safek that perhaps now it leavened, as Magen Avraham wrote in the name of the Bach seif katan 25 that we say perhaps now it softened — and so in Sefer Ateret Zekenim; nevertheless there is a practical difference whether the grain was found in matzah that remained in his hand after baking — there is no need for perhaps now it leavened; but perhaps it did not leaven — one can say. But this is safek of lack of knowledge, for we do not call that safek. However Shach wrote in siman 453 seif katan 15 in the name of responsum of Mishna Berurah that in safek sefeika even two safeks from lack of knowledge — nevertheless in pressing circumstances we rely on this safek sefeika; and the reason Shach wrote in his general rules of safek sefeika in siman 110: in pressing circumstances when there is safek sefeika, even if it can be clarified, one need not clarify — if so lack of knowledge in safek sefeika does not impede; whereas Peri Chadash wrote there that Shach contradicts his own words in siman 455 seif katan 4 — there is resolution; and Peri Chadash himself holds safek sefeika does not help where there is lack of knowledge — he follows his view that where it can be clarified in safek sefeika one must clarify:",

  "peri-megadim:1:א": "Introduction to siman 467:",
  "peri-megadim:1:ב": "First one must investigate siman of leavening in dough — when is it complete chametz and when stiff. And the sign of leavening in wheat and the five species of grain: what are they, and if whole wheat — is it complete chametz whenever it has a sign of leavening, or stiff? And behold in dough the Sages gave a measure like grasshopper horns — complete chametz liable for karet; if its face turned pale — it is stiff, as in siman 459 in Tur and Shulchan Aruch seif 2 and Pesachim 46a and 48b. And there is further stiff from fruit juice with water as in siman 462 — there we explained that pale face for us is d'rabbanan; but stiff from fruit juice with water where leavening is recognizable in them — one might say even according to those who say stiff it is in any amount; see there and nevertheless it is possible in Beit Yosef there is no practical difference regarding transgressing Pesach upon it — at end of siman 447. And it appears specifically mostly fruit juice with little water — behold mostly water more than half and little fruit juice — we follow the majority; and it has power to leaven; and if recognizable like grasshopper horns — liable for karet; but fruit juice alone without water — even if it stood all day and its cracks intermingled — that is not leavening in them. And Pesachim 38b: cooking leavens it, etc., meaning even within the measure of a mil in lukewarm water according to there. And it is difficult: say cooked in fruit juice alone without water — even if afterward water was mixed with it, still it does not leaven, for it is already cooked; and the law is the true law even for us in 462 in pressing circumstances permitted in fruit juice to cook — if afterward water is mixed it does not come to leavening; and in a whole grain so too one might say in pressing circumstances that we are not concerned it did not cook well — see Magen Avraham 463:4 and it will be explained from this inside. And for Rashi who explained fruit juice alone is stiff — if so. However cooked in fruit juice as it happens and afterward water is mixed — it appears it is complete chametz; and proof from 463 there: perhaps it did not cook well:",
  "peri-megadim:1:ג": "And if dough stood the measure of a mil even if there is no sign of leavening at all — it is complete chametz liable for karet, even though not found thus in explicit explanation — nevertheless such it is; and in my humble opinion chapter 5 siman 13 from pressing circumstances the measure of a mil is complete chametz; and so pale face is forbidden — it implies at first glance measure of a mil and pale face are one; on the contrary infer from the beginning the opposite in deaf dough: he measured with it the measure of a mil — as Magen Avraham wrote that grasshopper horns with making a sound are one; and from it the same applies to measure of a mil. And this is when he left it without occupation — behold if occupied all day even any occupation it does not leaven; and such is that of dripping dripping there 39b and Orach Chayyim 459. And this is all when signs of leavening were not born — if born, even pale face does not help — occupation in dough and grasshopper horns liable for karet; and from the ordinary case they said according to the majority: all who are occupied do not come to leavening. Also casting cold helps for dough as in siman 457 seif 2 — see Magen Avraham there 6; he left b'dieved uncertain — see end there letter 11; nevertheless certainly lukewarm like the heat of saliva does not nullify leavening. And behold casting cold helps even all day and even from above, for if he read on the first Yom Tov day of Pesach the casting of cold helps until the end of day 2; and even though soaked is like cooked and cooked leavens — this is an error, for for purging and absorption it is like cooked not for leavening; and so if he sees a sign of leavening casting cold does not nullify it, as stated:",
  "peri-megadim:1:ד": "However all this is in dough; but whole wheat and five species of grain upon which water came — it was not explained explicitly what is their clear sign to be complete chametz and liable for karet on them — only this alone we find in the words of the Sages Pesachim 40a: if wheat grains split open they are forbidden; if not split open — permitted — see there dispute of Shmuel and Mar Ukva. And if no sign of splitting arose and grains fell into water and did not stand the measure of a mil — it is obvious a whole grain is no worse than dough in such a case that there is no leavening; and so in Rosh chapter 30:40a in a split grain found in dough — since the dough did not leaven before a mil, so too the grain; and see end of siman and Magen Avraham letter 4 brought proof on this and it will be explained inside; but casting cold on a grain — one might say it does not help, for it enters all parts of the dough unlike a grain; but measure of a mil — obviously a grain is no worse than dough. And if a sign of leavening arose in whole grains — that is splitting — it must be judged whether they stood the measure of a mil from when water came upon them or did not stand a mil — if it is complete chametz clearly liable for karet or stiff. For us stiff is d'rabbanan; nevertheless without any splitting arising and the grains stood the measure of a mil from when water came upon them — it implies it is obviously not chametz at all; and proof from what Rabbenu Efrayim challenged from the incident of the ship that sank in a basket that Rava did not ask if they split open and needed to distinguish between resting at ease and placing — see Rosh chapter 30:40a there the grains stood more than a mil — we learn all that did not split open are permitted even if they stood the measure of a mil; and below I will explain further. And the Levush in seif 9 wrote: a cooked dish in which wheat and barley were found — even if they split open literally it is not complete chametz from the Torah but only complete chametz d'rabbanan; if not split open there is no complete chametz d'rabbanan — nevertheless people practice to forbid the cooked dish — see there; it implies even if they split open in cooking and split literally — nevertheless it is only chametz d'rabbanan; and in cooking less than a mil or less — see Penei Yehoshua Pesachim 39b. And behold it requires study; and from all codifiers it implies splitting is complete chametz; and the Rif would eat grains in their substance — it is a Torah safek whether Mar Ukva or Shmuel, and in any case split open literally it is complete chametz liable for karet in any amount even within measure of a mil — it implies; and so Rambam brought Mordechai chapter 30 and Bach in seif 2: grains that split open are complete chametz liable for karet — see there. However Maharam chapter 5 from pressing circumstances 13 and 18 wrote: grains that did not split open are not complete chametz in any amount — there is no proof to say behold they split open it is in any amount, for he holds stiff in any amount is forbidden and stiff is as one who sees there in chapters 4-5; nevertheless there too he equates Maharam's view with his teacher the Rif. And Magen Avraham 447:5 stiff is nullified in sixty — therefore a grain split open literally is stiff and forbids in any amount; and for us who are not expert whether it softened — forbidden:",
  "peri-megadim:1:ה": "From this manner we learn: whole grains upon which water came and they split open and stood the measure of a mil — in any case it is stiff chametz liable for karet. And what the Rosh wrote Pesachim 40b: a split grain in dough is safek chametz before measure of a mil or all the more so since he occupies it — since the dough did not leaven, so too the grain; unlike after measure of a mil split literally — complete chametz in any amount and liable for karet; and in a cooked dish less than measure of a mil and split literally — it is stiff in any amount liable for karet; and if they stood measure of a mil and did not split open — in this Rabbenu Efrayim distinguishes between placing and resting at ease — Magen Avraham 3-4 and Taz 3 that it is forbidden and still safek whether stiff forbidden within measure of a mil without splitting, or d'rabbanan; and whether casting cold helps or not — Magen Avraham letter 12 wrote to Rabbenu Efrayim: there is no certain chametz but safek chametz — see there and it will be explained inside regarding casting cold — see Tur in the name of Avi HaEzri and Beit Yosef there that the ship incident — one might say it was lifted from water; and Peri Chadash agreed: all that rest in water even many hours from hour to hour do not leaven (and that is without splitting) — and accordingly in pressing circumstances when resting in water and did not split even more than a mil — one might say to sell to a gentile on Pesach before it was lifted from water. And it will be explained inside seif 12 regarding a whole grain found in cold water that stood long without salting and brine — one might say to permit similarly in pressing circumstances. And see siman 453 in Magen Avraham 2 regarding types of herbs — see there:",
  "peri-megadim:1:ו": "And one may still question: whence this matter that the Sages said (Rif chapter 39 and Maharam chapter 5:13) a cooked dish with an unsplit grain is permitted — they brought Taz in seif 9 and behold from Pesachim 40a placing or even if stood not like Rabbenu Efrayim, or as Magen Avraham letter 12 wrote this is in water — here in cooking one cannot compare to cold water; and in Pesachim 39b cooked leavens and does not answer regarding whole grains that did not split — and still this requires study here; and it will be explained in Magen Avraham 12; I will explain further:",
  "peri-megadim:1:ז": "Know that baking that does not come to cooking — behold bread is baked chametz; and if afterward he put to flour with fruit juice — it is possible it leavens; and the baraita of bread that molded and was disqualified from a dog's feed is fit to leaven doughs — there is no proof, for there it speaks with water; and through the chametz it leavens quickly — see Magen Avraham letter 15 in the name of responsum of Rashba in what he wrote: and it is possible — and it will be explained there:",
  "peri-megadim:1:ח": "And know further that it is fitting to explain the matter of gravy that carries and releases from all of it and in all of it is every liquid thing — water, fruit juice, oil, and dissolved fat; everything cooked in it releases and absorbs throughout. Whence do I derive this? From this I derive it: Yoreh De'ah 104:2 — a mouse in soft fat that moves is like soaked — evidently fat too is called soaked; so too all the more cooked such; and even fat has pulsation — one authority says there is no permitted flavoring for prohibition; and nevertheless we learn everything that is like soaked is like cooked; and from the Torah it forbids until sixty in its non-species. And responsum Beit Yaakov siman 99 implies from his words: when there is a fingerbreadth on it to smear and a grain rests upon it — it is like soaked — requires study; one: Yoreh De'ah 105:61 in Shach 1 what is outside soaking is permitted; and in Taz 64 in gravy itself there is dispute whether it carries what is outside the gravy; and Pesachim 76b: smearing any amount is in the peel; and Yoreh De'ah 105:68 by fire — so it appears; and Magen Avraham 15 in the name of responsum of Rashba 176: a raw sheet like circumcision in salting also 110 degrees — and it will be explained there:",
  "peri-megadim:1:ט": "Further I will speak on a whole grain whose sign of splitting is leavening as stated: if found in fruit juice even with splitting we do not say presumably it leavened beforehand, for fruit juice is not accustomed to split; one in a thousand splits from fruit juice (and if it splits from fruit juice there is no splitting and like grasshopper horns in dough is a sign of leavening in them, for we hold fruit juice does not leaven). And at first glance from what the Ran wrote in responsum siman 59 regarding grains found split: there is safek from moisture of the stack it split, or from rain that fell on them at harvest — he answered it is far-fetched to attribute splitting to fruit juice — see there; and if so even if we say it occasionally happens, nevertheless we attribute to the common case; but in siman 462 seif 6 a grain in honey is permitted and stam implies even if split — nevertheless one might say without splitting and stood long therein like Rabbenu Efrayim in resting at ease: one, there was no reason to be stam; and further in cooked dish the Mechaber permits in any amount; and from what the Ran wrote there that it is not reasonable to attribute to stack moisture — namely what is common from stack moisture is not common in fruit juice such that it rests therein and splits. And responsum Tzitz Eliezer 123 and it will be explained inside Taz Yoreh De'ah I will explain further in an incident from this; and proof from what the Rosh wrote regarding a split grain in dough — here one cannot expand:",
  "peri-megadim:1:י": "And this grain in honey — even if there are not sixty b'dieved fruit juice does not leaven — see Levush there and Orach Chayyim Shabbos. And what is still fit to discuss — whether chametz is davar sheyeish lo matirin or not davar sheyeish lo matirin — see siman 447 and here and Levush and Yoreh De'ah 102 Shach 14 and Taz 13; and behold one might say leniently: since for us everything requires burning it is not davar sheyeish lo matirin; and accordingly on the last Yom Tov on the eighth hour of the day people practice to be lenient; and so in other things permitted to retain — one might say davar sheyeish lo matirin; and accordingly in 467 Taz 4 in safek sefeika permitted to retain and forbidden to eat because it is safek sefeika in davar sheyeish lo matirin — see Yoreh De'ah 110 Shach 57 from this; and here in seif 12 some say to permit in safek sefeika to eat for a time of need when one cannot wait on a roasted chicken — then safek sefeika is significant. And know safek sefeika in davar sheyeish lo matirin in its non-species requires study in it and in Shach there letter 57 — it implies somewhat the reason that anything in a thousand is not nullified — so too in safek sefeika — one might say — see there letter 56. However all that we are stringent from custom — if there is another safek one might say to permit like ready safek on the second Yom Tov — and it will be explained inside. Further one might say mixtures before Pesach in 467 seif 2 permitted to retain from safek sefeika and forbidden to eat — unlike what was made during Pesach since people practice to burn everything and forbidden to retain — we do not distinguish to permit retaining in safek sefeika and forbid eating like that of seif 12 in plucking the chickens — and this son:",
  "peri-megadim:1:כ": "We hold in Yoreh De'ah that a thing forbidden in any amount — we do not say in it: flavor is not flavor; practical difference for us: a pot with cooked dish and a split grain found — everything forbidden; and afterward Avi HaEzri placed it in an oven — this pot Avi HaEzri with dish and other pots — one might say it is aroma in any amount and purging permits — see Magen Avraham 447 letter 41 and it will be explained in Taz letter 17 in two any-amounts I will explain this:",
  "peri-megadim:1:ל": "In salting — see seif 14 and it will be explained there whether eaten because of its saltiness or not; and brine from salted meat even if eaten because of saltiness is considered boiling as in siman 91:65 in Yoreh De'ah. And if it fell on a vessel — it will be explained there with God's help. And if he left meat in a vessel that had a grain in brine, or left meat there and poured boiling water on them — see Yoreh De'ah 95 Shach letter 24 — and it will be explained below seif 14 from this; and here one cannot expand:",
  "peri-megadim:1:מ": "Or Taz and Magen Avraham who do not follow Orach Chayyim Shabbos who is concerned lest the gentile grind and sell to an Israelite — so too the Ran for his view that splitting is not permitted in grains at all placing — and he establishes in split ones unlike for us like Rabbenu Efrayim in seif 2 resting at ease; and so Rashi Pesachim 40a: these grains that are not recognizable, etc. — for if recognizable he would not challenge from kilayim to perhaps he will grind. And behold the Tur doubled his words and there is concern for leavening and it is not recognizable — one does not sell to a gentile lest he sell to an Israelite; since leavening is not recognizable — so in Levush; and it should have said grains that sank — one does not sell to a gentile if leavening is not recognizable, only at the beginning a leniency for an Israelite permitted without informing even if not recognizable; and for a gentile forbidden when not recognizable — behold if recognizable permitted for a gentile too; and one might say at first since not recognizable there is concern he will sell thus; but to be concerned he will grind and sell all this — not in concern for leavening; also for Rabbenu Efrayim there is no certain chametz only safek and perhaps only stiff — Magen Avraham 12 and letter 4 — except concern he will sell thus since not recognizable; and if they split open obviously forbidden for it is d'oraisa and we are concerned he will grind; leniency at beginning and end; and end of siman and Ateret Zekenim 1; and for what Chayyei Adam wrote: transgressing Beit Yosef — Rabbenu Efrayim there: nullified because not seen — see there. And end of siman 2: if he transgressed and kept and sowed after Pesach the shoots are permitted 463 letter 9 — requires study without splitting for it is only concern for leavening and safek whether stood measure of a mil — Magen Avraham 4; and perhaps stiff is all that did not split open at all — Magen Avraham 12; if so safek chametz after Pesach is d'rabbanan — see siman 448-449 and we wrote there from this. And do not err to explain חש״ש חימוץ as concern he will sell to Israelite — that stretch cannot be said, only חש״ש חימוץ not complete chametz as stated. And the Mechaber who omitted that it is not recognizable — it implies for a gentile in every case forbidden lest he grind flour; and for an Israelite a little is obvious permitted when recognizable; and 448 Taz and Magen Avraham agreed to permit when splitting recognizable even much to a gentile; and Magen Avraham 1-2 and it will be explained there further:",
  "peri-megadim:2:_": "And we inform you: see Turei Zahav Pesachim 40b and Maharam chapter 5:19 and as written here in Shulchan Aruch. And the Taz understood from the simple sense of Maharshal that he explained the words of Maharam thus in his version: keva keva stam — meaning both for Israelite and gentile; and for Israelite with informing and for gentile without informing because there is safek sefeika with a gentile unlike Israelite on the contrary there; there the prohibition for an Israelite is also with informing because perhaps a stumbling block will come from not informing — therefore they did not mention in the Gemara permission of much with informing; and on this the Taz questioned: certainly Maharam and the Mechaber hold specifically for a gentile a little; for an Israelite whether much or little with informing permitted specifically; and the Tur who wrote and it is reasonable like the author of the Ittur for his view that reads keva for Israelite. Avi HaEzri. However for halachah he agreed to Maharshal and as the Tur's version: for Israelite keva and with informing specifically, for we do not rely that he will eat before Pesach — for in presumption and majority too we do not rely wherever it can be fixed easily like that of R' Papa: ask him — behold much with informing not lest he forget to inform; and for a gentile little permitted because safek sefeika perhaps he will not sell to Israelite and perhaps Israelite will eat before Pesach; even l'chatchila one does not make safek sefeika in one's hands — this is preferable that presumably he will eat for his need. And there is a corruption in Taz — it should read: unlike when selling little to a gentile there is safek sefeika; and for Israelite much and specifically little with informing; and Bach seif 2 stringent as it appears for gentile little forbidden and for Israelite little with informing; and Netziv permits little to gentile as safek sefeika; and on the contrary — and it will be explained in Magen Avraham:",
  "peri-megadim:3:_": "For since Taz and Magen Avraham 4 brought the long Mordechai and it will be explained there; and behold what the Taz wrote to benefit from them together — meaning two by two not all together — see Yoreh De'ah 110:67; however in safek prohibition that became mixed and one fell to the sea one might say all permitted together too as safek sefeika perhaps there is no prohibition and perhaps the prohibition fell to the sea — see there and here in Magen Avraham. And what he wrote there is a leap in the Agur — Bach questioned thus; that Mordechai deals with recognizable splitting unlike here where those upon which rain fell remove them and the rest have safek sefeika; and he explained there: the question is as there permission if eating some is safek sefeika — so here in the middle of the grains there is safek sefeika all that removes the upper ones; and what the Taz wrote in any amount d'rabbanan requires study — those that became moist remove them and the rest safek whether rain fell on all or not. Also what he wrote in 453 if grinding permitted because does not return and revive — flour wet in wet; as Magen Avraham wrote there 6 and Taz there 2; and why here rule returns and revives, etc. — meaning why forbid eating too — requires study; and to say the difficulty is why take those that became moist and grind all before Pesach like grain that sprouted where there is sixty — there is effort to clarify and also b'dieved as there; unlike here where one should take those that became moist — one might say presumably rain did not fall on all the grain but safek perhaps fell on some and dried — for this he said if certainly there are sixty against safek that perhaps rain fell on some it is obvious permitted for eating and to grind before Pesach; and therefore safek — still difficulty: since it is safek why not permit eating like every safek sefeika; and the language is somewhat garbled; and what he wrote as there if sixty the reason is not to strengthen prohibition — we do not strengthen from safek sefeika but it deals presumptive prohibition and as there permitting eating from safek sefeika and here forbidding eating; and Magen Avraham 5 and Taz 5 and Peri Chadash here and in Darkei Moshe Florence edition I saw in gloss: Agur's law does not contradict Rashba's law — namely stacks are in the stalk and hard and upper parts of the stack like a roof and covering and recognizable rain did not reach the middle of the stack — see there:",
  "peri-megadim:4:א": "However from responsum of the Ran siman 59 brought in Shulchan Aruch seif 6 there is difficulty — except there too it implies the reason like Rashba's responsum that it is common when resting on sheaves rain drips on the upper parts of the stack and in the middle water does not reach — unlike the Agur incident of grain in an attic where there is safek in all of it — he properly said not to rely to permit for eating. And what the Rambam wrote in seif 12 to permit through safek sefeika — one might say in any amount d'rabbanan; but to eat grains in their substance not; and further one might say safek sefeika born on Pesach permits safek sefeika, for for us chametz is not called davar sheyeish lo matirin — if we forbid we must burn everything and to permit retaining and forbid eating is not seen; therefore we are lenient in everything unlike before Pesach where davar sheyeish lo matirin is such as to permit retaining and not eating — we wrote this in the introduction. And for halachah the Taz wrote in pressing circumstances permitted; and Chayyei Adam 10 and Magen Avraham 2 questioned two by two; and here concern perhaps rain fell on all; and see Orach Chayyim Shabbos what he wrote that safek sefeika like majority and chametz in any amount they did not follow majority; and until nightfall safek sefeika requires distinction between that of seif 12 — see there; and safek sefeika that reverses — see Yoreh De'ah 110 on this and it will be explained in letter 5 on this:",
  "peri-megadim:4:ב": "And indeed from responsum of the Ran siman 59 it is difficult — except there too it implies the reason like Rashba that it is common in sheaves that rain drips on upper parts of the stack and in the middle water does not reach — unlike in the Agur incident. And what the Mechaber wrote in seif 3 if their appearance did not change — behold if changed forbidden — Avi HaEzri and Levush also forbidden to retain them — behold they did not come in a ship; even if appearance changed if there are no others one might say permitted:",
  "peri-megadim:5:א": "There is Taz: behold flour in honey without moistening is like siman 462:6 except only for eating thus; but to make a drink from the start — even flour in honey all that initially lacks sixty then leavens in water before cooking well; however before Pesach permitted and nullified in sixty with water; and from the fifth hour until nightfall if known flour was mixed or small vessels customary to mix flour and concern perhaps no sixty against flour or perhaps in one place not blended well — behold for us flavor is not flavor in other prohibitions; and soaked in soft honey is like soaked — see Yoreh De'ah 105 and 104; if so honey became nevelah and afterward needs sixty against all the honey and there are not sixty in water against all the honey for it is Yom Tov and enhanced. Therefore for one who permits small vessels to make a drink from them before the fifth hour — from the fifth until nightfall not from what we said; however from not nullifying prohibition permitted all where there is safek whether they mix — similar to vinegar — see Yoreh De'ah 114 Shach 21 and 116:28 — see there; except from nevelah such — perhaps fine flour or flour mixed with water as he wrote; and b'dieved if cooked thus one might say in pressing circumstances if not known — perhaps we do not say flavor is not flavor l'chatchila must cook before fifth hour; and large hive barrels not customary to forge — even rooms not recognizable — Taz's view implies permission even to eat during Pesach that honey; and so to cook during Pesach from those large barrels not customary to forge — as appears from Rambam's conclusion that not to eat only from the hive in a barrel — implies permitted to eat on Pesach from it and so drink if fit for drinking on Yom Tov else no melacha of another's Yom Tov. And behold what he proved permitted to make drink on Pesach from large barrels — though obvious since even for eating Rambam permits — all the more so drink — one might say drink is stricter lest flour inside and not blended well and Yom Tov in water in any amount on Pesach unlike eating in their substance fruit juice does not leaven — therefore he wrote no concern at all in large barrels where they never mix flour:",
  "peri-megadim:5:ב": "And what he wrote regarding saffron — at first since we see saffron dissolved in the cooked dish and nothing remains — necessarily even if nevelah meat threads are mixed custom is to invert and all becomes like saffron — therefore we follow present state; somewhat strained to explain Taz's words; also there is concern Yom Tov — see there; and Magen Avraham letter 3 and Chayyei Adam here letter 16 wrote novel fat that did not change to a permitted thing — nevertheless one might say we do not permit forbidden fat for it is not such a change as blood which the Torah forbade and did not change to another appearance — and blood that was cooked is permitted for most codifiers and so is the halachah unlike forbidden fat; no verse needed — see there. And if known flour or fine flour was put in honey and fell into a dish in less than sixty — Chayyei Adam relying on leniency of Rabbenu Yonah that it turned to honey did not lose — see there and Magen Avraham letter 11 — meaning when it stood some time in the honey — for in short time it does not turn to honey:",
  "peri-megadim:6:א": "And know in Yoreh De'ah 84 and Shach letter 37 honey is accustomed to preserve and sustain things hidden in it; as Rashba wrote in responsum 80: whole things preserve; like Bava Batra regarding Herod who hid seven years in honey; and cut things quickly consumed — see there; therefore whole grains found in honey on Pesach even though long time — soaked preserves; and soaked in soft honey honey becomes chametz and forbids the dish in any amount — no distinction whether there was living soul in it or one grain unlike fine flour long time turned to honey — permitted b'dieved as written. And it will be explained in Magen Avraham 7:",
  "peri-megadim:6:ב": "And figs — Taz wrote flour — one might say fruit juice and fine flour concern — see Levush; and in my humble opinion zafarin too forbidden lest substitution; and see Levush: honey from a barrel is common and we are not concerned for substitution unlike zafarin in my view not common — they say it came from overseas; and so dried fruits from Israel people buy known and common to dry for Pesach and no substitution. And fruit a gentile dried with chametz bread in one oven — from aroma one might say b'dieved forbid the mixture — we hold bread aroma does not forbid — see siman 447; nevertheless one might say touched chametz bread. And raisins red — not eaten; small black ones some permit — see there and Magen Avraham letter 8 and it will be explained there:",
  "peri-megadim:6:ג": "And know in honey and wine if it stood long — one might say split from wine and honey and permitted unlike frying in fat which sense testifies splitting does not occur from fat frying — if found split in the rounds one might say common and attribute from elsewhere; but in softened alone we permit for us — as stated; and it will be explained letter 12; and in Mishnah Berurah regarding roasted chicken with grain found — also:",
  "peri-megadim:7:_": "And figs — Taz wrote flour; fruit juice and fine flour concern; Levush; zafarin forbidden lest substitution; dried fruit from Israel not substituted:",
  "peri-megadim:8:א": "And tsuker kandil — Taz wrote in sour vats; stam vessels Avi HaEzri and Yom Tov against Yom Tov is from siman 447 from this; and end of siman; and what tsuker Candia they eat on last Yom Tov — hot tsuker without real cooking; experts cook in special vessels — product is cane grown in ground boiled in water extracted like flour — so merchants said; Magen Avraham letter 9; l'chatchila practice to sell even hot tsuker; b'dieved not forbidden; even our kandil cooked in sour vats if stood — not forbidden after Pesach for nevelah ben nevelah; Avi HaEzri siman 447 there. And Taz: not common among us — properly concerned for substitution like figs from his garden; and in my view zafarin letter 9 and 7 — recognizable distinct form:",
  "peri-megadim:8:ב": "And if pressing circumstances on Pesach market day and much selling tsuker — one might say permitted to sell hot tsuker on pressing circumstances; merchants testified no flour mixed in hot tsuker — everything by custom; where practice prohibition do not permit before them; where practice permission permitted. Now Israelites make hot tsuker for Pesach with some change lest substitution — well done and fixed per great ones of the generation and Peri Chadash. See Levush: some lenient in kandil tsuker; on last Yom Tov do not be so stringent — see there:",
  "peri-megadim:9:_": "Saffron — Taz and Levush; see letter 7 distinction between common here and not common — concerned:",
  "peri-megadim:10:א": "Even Taz in the name of Bach there in seif 11 that softened — at least require; reason some stringent concern to rule like Mar Ukva Pesachim 40a all that placed on opening of roaster and would split — we are not expert; but not softened hard ones we are expert — no leavening reached — see there and Peri Chadash here. And it appears to me: only when there are sixty — any amount d'rabbanan; but without sixty like eating grains in their substance — Torah safek for all who say d'oraisa; and in seif 2 grain sunk in river though not split forbidden — Rava did not ask splitting — also did not ask eating in their substance forbidden Torah safek; all the more in hot cooked dish hard kernels not permitted even without sixty — Magen Avraham letter 12. However if grain fell into dish above boiling — hilat — see 454 Magen Avraham 4: if very hard even without sixty lenient — ruling authority must be careful. And regarding benefit: softened not split — Taz letter 15 implies forbidden in one safek for benefit burn all; Ateret Zekenim letter 18 agreed forbidden for benefit; Eshel Avraham letter 26 leaned b'dieved to permit benefit — requires study:",
  "peri-megadim:10:ב": "An incident occurred in our community K'K Kamerani: they made round loaves called knaidlach from ground matzah kneaded with goose fat and salt and pepper without any water and fried in fat rising on all sides; afterward placed in a bowl and poured boiling gravy from kli rishon; found in one a softened grain; they asked me the law of the food and vessels on Pesach:",
  "peri-megadim:10:ג": "Answer: softened alone grain not split — food appears permitted; what concern from fruit juice with eggs does not leaven; little salt even in our place since form changed does not leaven — see 462 Magen Avraham 6 goose fat after melting water of rinsing gone — see there; 463 Magen Avraham 1; and for us b'dieved permitted — see there; 462 pressing circumstances — see there; pepper without water does not leaven — see 455 Magen Avraham 19; 462 salt specifically water significant. Pouring gravy did not reach middle of rounds; pouring 110 degrees works; even for chametz all the more; nevertheless did not leaven in brief moment by gravy that did not reach inside — discard that bread with the grain not the rest. If split grain found in one — judge: splitting sign of leavening; fruit juice does not leaven — perhaps before leavening though we do not strengthen prohibition such case impaired — nevertheless Mechaber 462:6 grain in honey does not forbid — therefore split here 467:9 permits cooked dish without splitting — strained to say taste even without sixty in honey — Magen Avraham 12; see Tzitz Eliezer 123; not impossible split from fruit juice and egg dough swelled perhaps like grasshopper horns more in fruit juice — no chametz; in water that is sign. In seif 11 split grain in dough Rambam forbids dough even without standing — attribute leavened beforehand — splitting impaired presumption; Rosh Pesachim 40a if dough did not leaven so grain; we do not say leavened beforehand; possible splitting in grain not clear sign; for us splitting complete chametz — so in fruit juice too; not impossible split from fruit juice; Ran responsum brought here seif 6 (responsum 59) not from stack moisture — little; rests in fruit juice many days splits; examiner in Peri Chadash seif 16 incident Tzitz 123 grain in goose fat — he expanded there: in honey and wine if stood long perhaps split from wine and honey permitted unlike fat frying — if split in rounds common attribute from outside; softened alone we permit; Magen Avraham 12; 462:6; Mishnah Berurah roasted chicken:",
  "peri-megadim:10:ד": "If placed rounds in bowl cut and poured gravy found softened grain — forbid pouring; all the more stringent without measure of a mil in hot no measure. Even already cooked in fat perhaps not cooked well; especially if cut grain and white flour seen — not cooked well — see 463 Magen Avraham 4. In introduction we questioned Pesachim 39b cooked leavens — not established in fruit juice then water does not leaven; for Rashi stiff fits; Ran responsum 59 such is fruit juice and water — stiff nullified in sixty; Magen Avraham letter 21 will explain:",
  "peri-megadim:11:א": "On Yom Tov — Taz for Mechaber in 447:10 permitted; for us custom to forbid — obvious permitted to retain on eighth all the more from any amount. But less than sixty forbidden to retain — Magen Avraham 14. General rule: all permitted to retain — permitted to sell and benefit; but some forbidden to retain permitted to sell as Mechaber wrote; see Levush here. And since Taz did not learn only a fortiori — implies other two d'rabbanan like stiff less than sixty (Magen Avraham 447:5, Taz 19) or dry in dry in its species in majority or species in wet less than sixty; and imagine burned Pesach wine with chametz wine (though essence not its species we follow taste for leniency and stringency — see 98 Shach 6; until nightfall wine for its taste and smells in thousand — decree unlike not its species) — forbidden to retain on eighth of Pesach; and what more from any amount one might rely on questions, etc. However Levush wrote last Yom Tov d'rabbanan and retaining also d'rabbanan — two d'rabbanan they were not stringent — implies every two d'rabbanan do not combine in retaining. However other days even two d'rabbanan purging and any amount or other two d'rabbanan — do not learn from eighth of Pesach; see 447 Magen Avraham letter 41. And know prohibition of retaining in half measure is still stiff; all the more in wet in its non-species less than sixty that reverted to prohibition — palate tastes and tastes — for all perhaps still stiff; Peri Chadash here and Magen Avraham 447:42 and 44. And if nullified by mouth and said all chametz I will have on Pesach is nullified — for those who can nullify even what did not yet exist — see 446 Magen Avraham 1 and responsum of Moreinu from Lubin and responsum Bach; nevertheless if less than sixty on eighth in its non-species forbidden to retain for retaining d'rabbanan — nevertheless if not nullified d'oraisa also for Rashbatz in Maamar Yavin Shemuah and so Maharan beginning retaining stiff because of eating prohibition though stiff nullified suffices — nevertheless close to Torah prohibition. And it will be explained Magen Avraham 18:",
  "peri-megadim:11:ב": "And know roasted by fire forbidden from chametz — so it appears — see Yoreh De'ah 105 distinctions: where sixty required by law no removal needed; where stringency required sixty and by law amount of removal — on eighth such forbidden to retain must remove as it appears and discard — rest permitted to retain. And if nullified by mouth until nightfall forbidden as it appears; and afterward cooked in pot before nightfall even if sixty in piece against prohibition — as it appears we say flavor is not flavor — see 96 Shach 9 — then needs sixty against removal; forbidden for purging does not apply to food — see 106 there Taz and Shach 1-2. Therefore piece forbidden by law on eighth by amount of removal — transgressed and retained after Pesach and cooked in kli rishon afterward — all without sixty against removal — depends on dispute 447 Magen Avraham 44 whether after Pesach needs sixty or less than sixty also not acquired. There we wrote one might say only nevelah through d'rabbanan transgressed — therefore for all no sixty needed against absorption; perhaps permitted reverted to prohibition — same for Beit Yosef; perhaps Beit Yosef no palate tastes. Understand this — requires study; end of siman I will write more laws scattered to this; in opening beginning of book part 3 we expanded laws pertaining to this siman:",
  "peri-megadim:11:ג": "And know on eighth of Pesach in complete chametz or without sixty — even though did not transgress stiff Beit Yosef for we are expert in fixed month — nevertheless R' Shimon acquires after Pesach as explained Tur 448 bread chametz gift to Israelite — two days equal lest come to treat lightly — see 448. And even if he made dough on eighth of Pesach and saw no time to bake unless it leavened first and preceded and nullified and transgressed afterward and retained — we do not say two d'rabbanan such as written; Magen Avraham 447 letter 46. And what was questioned how burn on Yom Tov and also seventh of Pesach how burn — see 446 Magen Avraham letter 2 and responsum Chatam Sofer; Magen Avraham 14 will explain; responsum Penei Yehoshua part 1:15; also Magen Avraham 14 with God's help:",
  "peri-megadim:12:_": "And so Taz's holy words need explanation: Rosh Pesachim page 40 on that which split open, etc., wrote: split grain in matzah — one might say amount of removal not needed since splitting is not complete chametz only close, etc.; and since dough did not leaven so too grain; we are not concerned perhaps grain was in water — waters are accustomed to guard; some forbid that matzah and some forbid those baked with it — not clear; so Rambam wrote cut place of pulsation — see there brought in Tur. If so his conclusion: in safek chametz no removal needed; and Rambam in recognizable sour grain — removal; in roasting and baking and Beit Yosef implies Rosh agreed finally split is sign of leavening; as Maharif — Torah safek, etc.; as in introduction split grain complete chametz; therefore Chullin retaining in water necessarily or in flour already leavened beforehand and forbids amount of removal in baking — in dough forbids nothing in cold per his view. Bach questioned from Rosh that waters guard — implies sour grain forbids all water; below wrote chametz in cold does not forbid — no taste in cold (brought seif 12 some forbid during Pesach) — resolved: here during Pesach properly if was in water something purged and forbids all its water from water — see there. Mechaber brought Rosh for halachah: split grain in dough or baking removal — even in dough or from distress of kneading where grain touched pulsates removal from split grain in dough not more for waters guard them; therefore fell at kneading or flour grain was and to lessen time when water poured to knead we are not concerned waters leavened only in kneading grain pierced and amount of removal suffices. Some forbid all dough or that matzah — appears Rashba responsum 176 brought in Beit Yosef forbids all dough not that matzah alone from two reasons: grain touched through stirring in all dough (in cold too from distress of kneading something purges) and waters carry taste in all — see there; said some forbid all dough Rashba and Tur in others all matzot (not baked only what kneaded in water Avi HaEzri) or that matzah Tur in some great ones — here found here was in kneading and through its stirring touched all dough in hand that matzah; Magen Avraham 16; see Beer HaGolah; understand. Rambam glossed during Pesach forbid; before Pesach not — appears Taz intent: during Pesach concerned grain in cold waters purged something forbids all dough; before Pesach any amount nullified as seif 12 Taz 16 — unless what distinction between dough or matzah; Magen Avraham 15-16 if found in one matzah in hand here fell forbidden in cold during Pesach through compression pressing purges in cold; if in whole dough all dough forbidden — during Pesach in any amount; before Pesach nullified what fell in sixty until measure. Taz answered Rosh Avi HaEzri Derishah Levush Darkei Moshe there Rosh holds grain in cold forbidden grain does not forbid water (reverse Avi HaEzri in Tur explained inside seif 12) — if was in water grain leavened afterward through compression of kneading cold dough forbidden from what purged in such case. See letter 16. What Rambam ruled from Maharil and Maharash already written; what softened though not split for us and Rambam who did not gloss seif 11-12-14 relied seif 9; Peri Chadash 16 leaned b'dieved if not split only softened perhaps Rambam wrote custom but b'dieved rely on Mechaber; if so softened b'dieved perhaps permitted eating — requires study; benefit perhaps even dissenters seif 9 here permitted; Taz 17 Rokeach see there; if in water safek stood above — b'dieved perhaps forbidden; Maharil incident knaidlach letter 12; 462:6; goose fat; Peri Chadash 16:",
  "peri-megadim:13:א": "If Taz: see seif 8:11 Shach 27 and 105 Taz 4 all the more forbid without pressing circumstances for it purges and absorbs unlike together; siman 447 Magen Avraham 9; 454 letter 3 and 13 see there; chametz in any amount forbids more; one hard grain to chicken if Yom Tov in water; before Pesach equals other prohibitions; this plucking — though Yoreh De'ah 88:11 forbids l'chatchila in any amount all the more here salted on feathers rinsed plucked in any amount:",
  "peri-megadim:13:ב": "Avi HaEzri end of siman brought Mordechai: plucked chicken on mouth of chametz vessel — forbade chicken — see there; Chayyei Adam letter 33; indeed until nightfall in any amount should permit; pouring only 110 degrees until nightfall; perhaps this authority until nightfall in any amount even for those who purify and absorb unlike Ran; Magen Avraham 454:3; Chayyei Adam 8; even Beit Yosef permit; all the more Avi HaEzri; Eshel Avraham; Eshel Avraham 14:95 letter 24:",
  "peri-megadim:14:א": "Forbidden Taz Magen Avraham 16 Chayyei Adam; also from time to time we establish prohibition; Tur incident plucked — Rabbenu Raavan forbade — did not mention splitting; Mechaber for his method seif 9 specifically split; softened even in kli rishon in any amount dish permitted; meaning before boiling before chicken boiled (not in cold from place to place we do not establish prohibition); align Taz with Magen Avraham distinguish time and place; see 16-17; will explain. Taz: do not question safek perhaps now fell digested in chicken — split in whole grain not digested; what perhaps did not leaven hard — for us split clear chametz Magen Avraham 15:",
  "peri-megadim:14:ב": "If softened alone not split — food permitted; fruit juice with eggs; little salt form changed; 462:6; 463:1 b'dieved; 462 pressing; pepper; gravy did not reach middle; 110 degrees; discard one bread; split — fruit juice; 462:6; 467:9; knaidlach; Rosh; Ran; Peri Chadash; letter 12:",
  "peri-megadim:14:ג": "Further Mechaber lenient softened in any amount one safek d'rabbanan lenient; here one safek stringent; perhaps time to time worse establish on presumption lack of knowledge in rabbinic safek lenient Yoreh De'ah 105 safek soaked; Kaf HaChayyim Shach 1 in any amount perhaps we say it is different; therefore same for other meat:",
  "peri-megadim:15:_": "Split grain Taz for Mechaber seif 9 even one safek in dish in any amount without split dish permitted Magen Avraham 12 all the more safek sefeika; except Rambam agrees here one safek forbids benefit not only softened unlike Chayyei Adam letter 10; split grain for us complete chametz no safek unlike Rosh Pesachim split grain; see seif 11 Peri Chadash end Magen Avraham:",
  "peri-megadim:16:_": "If Taz as letter 12 cold does not purge and absorb at all even any amount; Rosh waters guard else grain sour forbids water; appears his view; see 17; Levush 11-12; examiner Tur Bach sees Avi HaEzri disputes Tur: sour grain in cold waters nogeia possible even cold; Tur reverse: grain sour in cold waters no taste in water; Avi HaEzri there split, etc.; Mechaber split grain or chametz bread permitted during Pesach — Tur view even Pesach no purging in cold; some forbid — Rosh waters guard Taz 12; if before Pesach and who is anonymous permitting even during; Avi HaEzri did not permit chametz bread anyway forbids; Beer HaGolah letter 70 requires study; Magen Avraham 19 will explain:",
};
